WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.006
I'm Modulus.

00:00:02.006 --> 00:00:06.299
Tencent's Hy4 flash stores two hundred and twenty billion parameters, and a

00:00:06.299 --> 00:00:09.161
quarter of them are never multiplied by anything.

00:00:09.161 --> 00:00:10.592
They are looked up.

00:00:12.183 --> 00:00:16.866
Sixty-three billion of them sit in four Engram tables, outside the experts.

00:00:16.866 --> 00:00:21.159
A token reads sixty-four rows of those tables, about twelve kilobytes.

00:00:21.579 --> 00:00:25.081
Everything a token actually computes with comes to about eleven billion.

00:00:25.081 --> 00:00:28.901
So the big number is a storage number, not a compute number.

00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:33.030
Take the word Great, after Alexander the.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.121
Each token id is first swapped for a canonical id.

00:00:36.541 --> 00:00:40.671
The, capital The, all-caps THE, even the Vietnamese word with accents, all

00:00:40.671 --> 00:00:42.048
fold into one id.

00:00:42.048 --> 00:00:44.458
The vocabulary shrinks by about a quarter.

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:47.563
Each id is multiplied by its own odd constant.

00:00:47.563 --> 00:00:50.546
XOR the last two and you have the bigram key.

00:00:50.546 --> 00:00:53.530
XOR in the third and you have the trigram key.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:57.554
Eight heads take the bigram key modulo eight different primes, and eight

00:00:57.554 --> 00:00:59.956
more do the same with the trigram key.

00:00:59.956 --> 00:01:01.458
Each remainder is a row.

00:01:01.878 --> 00:01:04.207
Those sixteen rows are all this layer reads.

00:01:04.207 --> 00:01:07.700
Nothing in the address depends on the hidden state, so it is

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.156
known before the layer runs.

00:01:10.825 --> 00:01:12.375
The design is DeepSeek's Engram.

00:01:12.375 --> 00:01:16.095
The rows from every head are joined, and the hidden state gates

00:01:16.095 --> 00:01:18.265
them before they reach the residual stream.

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:21.049
Hy4 flash keeps the hashing and the gate.

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:24.656
Like DeepSeek's own release, it drops the convolution at the end, and

00:01:24.656 --> 00:01:26.760
it adds a twist to the gate.

00:01:28.392 --> 00:01:32.059
Because the address needs only token ids, I computed all sixty-four of

00:01:32.059 --> 00:01:33.281
them on a laptop.

00:01:33.681 --> 00:01:36.752
Each table sits alone in a shard, so a row is at

00:01:36.752 --> 00:01:38.800
a fixed byte offset in Tencent's own files.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.561
Sixty-four range requests brought back twelve thousand two hundred and eighty-eight bytes.

00:01:43.561 --> 00:01:46.105
Lower-case great lands on the same rows.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.523
Here is the formula.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.695
Each of the four residual streams computes a gate from its own

00:01:52.695 --> 00:01:54.343
hidden state and the memory's key.

00:01:54.763 --> 00:01:58.244
Then comes something the paper does not have: a learned offset per

00:01:58.244 --> 00:01:59.695
stream, added to the gate.

00:02:00.115 --> 00:02:02.171
The sum scales one shared value vector.

00:02:02.171 --> 00:02:06.283
If the offset is large, the gate can no longer switch the memory off.

00:02:07.775 --> 00:02:11.154
At the first Engram layer the offsets are tiny, so the gate

00:02:11.154 --> 00:02:13.126
works as the paper intends, a switch.

00:02:13.546 --> 00:02:18.155
At the last one, stream zero's offset is about minus one point one.

00:02:18.155 --> 00:02:21.700
Its multiplier never reaches zero, so the memory always arrives.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.368
I tested that on a CPU.

00:02:25.368 --> 00:02:28.125
With the tables off, perplexity rises seventeen percent.

00:02:28.125 --> 00:02:30.537
With the rows scrambled, it nearly doubles.

00:02:30.537 --> 00:02:35.018
Wrong memory hurts more than none, because the gate cannot shut it out.

00:02:36.617 --> 00:02:38.386
How much to spend on memory?

00:02:38.386 --> 00:02:41.925
The paper found loss is lowest with three quarters to four fifths

00:02:41.925 --> 00:02:43.695
of the idle budget in experts.

00:02:44.075 --> 00:02:48.081
Hy4 flash puts sixty-nine point four percent there, a little further toward

00:02:48.081 --> 00:02:51.420
memory, on a curve measured at a much smaller scale.

00:02:52.675 --> 00:02:56.352
Quantize the rest of the model and the tables become a bigger

00:02:56.352 --> 00:02:57.884
share of what you download.

00:02:57.884 --> 00:03:00.028
The FP8 release keeps them in sixteen-bit.

00:03:01.775 --> 00:03:04.890
The transformers port keeps the tables off the accelerator.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:09.765
The llama dot cpp port hashes every layer first and prefetches the rows.

00:03:10.185 --> 00:03:13.654
And on a Mac with a hundred and twenty-eight gigabytes, the table

00:03:13.654 --> 00:03:15.967
stays on the SSD while the model runs.

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:19.450
Eleven billion parameters do the math.

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:23.300
Sixty-three billion only need an address, and somewhere cheap to live.

00:03:25.192 --> 00:03:28.471
Hash the tokens, gate the rows, and keep the tables on the host.

00:03:29.792 --> 00:03:31.944
Every source is in the full article.

00:03:31.944 --> 00:03:32.558
I'm Modulus.

00:03:32.558 --> 00:03:32.866
Bye!
